\pard\qc\b\fs32 The 'Japa' Phenomenon and Human Capital Flight in Nigeria\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qc\b\fs32 A Public Health Analysis of Youth Migration Intentions, Mental Health, and Implications for National Development\b0\fs24\par
\par
\pard\qc Mabrig Korie\par
\pard\qc Mabrig Research Institute (MRI)\par
\pard\qc contact@mabrigkorie.org | mabrigkorie.org\par
\par
\pard\qc Mabrig Journal of Interdisciplinary Research (MJIR)\par
\pard\qc Volume 1, Issue 1, Article 002, pp. 21-40 (2024)\par
\pard\qc DOI: 10.5678/mjir.v1i1.002\par
\par
\pard\qj\b\fs28\sb240\sa120 Abstract\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Nigeria is living through a wave of outward migration that has acquired its own name: "Japa." Most of the public conversation about it has stayed inside the language of economics and politics \u8212? the naira, unemployment, insecurity, the cost of living. Far less attention has gone to what this movement means for public health and for the country's long-term human capital. This paper looks at migration intentions among young Nigerians, the mental health currents running underneath those intentions, and the wider consequences of losing skilled professionals for Nigeria's development trajectory.\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Drawing on national survey data, official health-workforce records, and recent published literature, we examine how economic uncertainty, mental well-being, social media exposure, and perceptions of opportunity shape the decision to leave among Nigerians aged 18 to 35. The evidence is consistent: the desire to emigrate is widespread, and for many young people leaving has become the most realistic route to security and a livable future. The departure of doctors, nurses, teachers, and engineers is already placing measurable strain on service delivery, institutional capacity, and the country's longer-run economic prospects.\par
\pard\qj\sa160 We argue that Japa needs to be read as more than a migration or economic story \u8212? it is also a public health and human capital challenge. Responding to it will require coordinated investment in mental health support, meaningful work, stronger institutions, better working conditions, and a credible reason for young people to believe in Nigeria's development prospects again. Retaining talent is not optional for a country trying to build a sustainable future; it is a precondition for one.\par
\pard\qj\sa160 {\b Keywords:} Japa, Migration, Human Capital Flight, Public Health, Mental Health, Youth Development, Brain Drain, Nigeria, Workforce Retention, National Development.\par
\pard\qj\b\fs28\sb240\sa120 1. Introduction: A Word That Became a Generation's Verdict\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Nigeria is in the middle of something that feels both familiar and new. Young people are leaving the country in numbers that have not been seen in recent memory. The word on the street and online is "Japa" \u8212? a Yoruba expression that means to flee or escape quickly. What began as casual slang has become the dominant way an entire generation talks about its future. It is no longer just about a few ambitious individuals chasing greener pastures abroad. It has turned into a collective movement, a quiet but powerful statement about how many young Nigerians see their prospects at home.\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Recent national surveys have put hard numbers to this feeling. The 2024 LEAP Africa Japa Report found that 71 percent of young Nigerians between 18 and 35 were actively considering leaving the country. Even more striking, 85 percent said they would migrate if they had a realistic opportunity. Polling from Afrobarometer and similar organizations shows comparable patterns, with intention especially high among the educated, those living in cities, and people in the prime of their working lives. The preferred destinations have stayed remarkably steady for years \u8212? the United Kingdom, Canada, and the United States continue to top the list, and many of those with firm plans hope to leave within the next year or two.\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Most of the public conversation about Japa has stayed within the language of economics and politics. People point to unemployment, the collapse of the naira, rising insecurity, poor infrastructure, and a general loss of confidence in governance. These factors are real and powerful; they shape daily life for millions of young people trying to build something. But they do not tell the whole story. What often gets missed is the human and health dimension \u8212? the mental exhaustion, the slow erosion of hope, and the way constant exposure to images of "better lives" abroad reshapes what young people believe is possible for them at home. There is also a downstream effect on the systems that hold the country together, especially the health system.\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Between 2019 and 2024, thousands of Nigerian-trained doctors and nurses left for opportunities abroad. The Medical and Dental Council of Nigeria recorded peaks of nearly three thousand doctors requesting verification letters in a single year. The United Kingdom alone has taken in large numbers of Nigerian health professionals. The practical result is a doctor-to-patient ratio that has worsened further, moving even farther from the World Health Organization's recommended standard. Remaining staff are stretched thin, and the loss of experienced hands shows up in longer waits, heavier workloads, and quiet conversations in hospital corridors about who might be next to leave.\par
\pard\qj\sa160 This paper approaches the Japa phenomenon through a public health lens. We are interested in migration intentions among young Nigerians, the mental health factors that travel alongside those intentions, and what the large-scale departure of skilled professionals means for the country's health systems and broader development. We draw on recent national surveys, official workforce statistics, and emerging literature to build a clearer picture. Our argument is straightforward: Japa is not only an economic or migration story. It is also a public health and human capital challenge, and understanding it this way opens up different and more useful ways of thinking about what needs to happen next.\par
\pard\qj\b\fs28\sb240\sa120 2. Methods: Reading the Available Evidence Together\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa160 This study draws together existing high-quality data rather than collecting new primary information. We combined findings from recent national surveys, official administrative records on health workforce movement, and relevant published literature. The main sources included the LEAP Africa Japa Report (2024), Afrobarometer polling on migration attitudes, data from the Medical and Dental Council of Nigeria and the Nursing and Midwifery Council of Nigeria covering 2018 to 2024, and supporting reports from the International Organization for Migration and Nigeria's National Bureau of Statistics.\par
\pard\qj\sa160 We focused on young people aged 18 to 35 because this group sits at the center of the current wave and represents the largest share of current and future human capital. We looked at both the scale of stated migration intention and the factors most often linked to it \u8212? economic conditions, mental well-being, social media exposure, and perceptions of future opportunity in Nigeria. Where the data allowed, we examined differences across regions, levels of education, employment status, and whether respondents worked in healthcare.\par
\pard\qj\sa160 For the health workforce piece, we tracked official verification and migration numbers over recent years, with special attention to doctors and nurses. We also reviewed Nigeria's National Policy on Health Workforce Migration, introduced in 2024, to understand the current policy direction. Our approach combined straightforward descriptive summaries with a narrative synthesis that tried to connect individual decisions to larger system-level consequences, paying particular attention to mental health indicators where they appeared and to the voices of young people themselves as captured in the qualitative parts of the source reports.\par
\pard\qj\b\fs28\sb240\sa120 3. Results: A Consistent and Sobering Picture\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa160 The picture that comes through is consistent across sources. Recent national surveys put the share of Nigerians who say they have thought seriously about leaving, or are actively planning to, somewhere between 56 and 71 percent overall \u8212? and often higher among younger adults. The LEAP Africa 2024 data stands out: 71 percent of respondents aged 18\u8211?35 were considering relocation, and 85 percent indicated they would go if a realistic opportunity appeared. More than half of those with concrete plans hoped to leave in 2024 or 2025, and the top destination countries have stayed remarkably stable over time.\par
\pard\qj\sa160 What pushes these intentions is rarely a single cause. Economic hardship, unemployment or underemployment, and the daily difficulty of making ends meet sit at the top of most lists. But two other threads appear with surprising regularity. One is a deep sense of mental and emotional exhaustion \u8212? a feeling that "there is simply no future here." The other is the powerful role of social media and peer networks. Many young people describe seeing constant images of friends or acquaintances who have left and seem to be doing well. This creates both useful information and a kind of social pressure; some say openly that they do not want to be the only one left behind while everyone else moves on.\par
